<iscomment>
    Places one banner slot on a listing page.

    Reads four page scoped variables the including template sets just above it:

      tsSlotId       the configured slot to render
      tsSlotClass    extra classes for the wrapper, or empty
      tsCategoryId   category being browsed, or empty
      tsSearchQuery  shopper search term, or empty

    tsSlotClass exists because the side banner has to be a direct child of the column to be
    sticky - a sticky element can only travel inside its own parent, so wrapping it in a
    responsive div sized to the banner would pin it in place. The responsive class goes on
    this wrapper instead.

    Empty context parameters are passed through rather than omitted, because building the URL
    conditionally would need script scope and page variables only reach an included template
    through expressions. The controller reads a blank parameter as absent.

    The banner arrives through a remote include rather than being auctioned in the page, so
    the listing page keeps whatever caching it has today - see the page cache notes in the
    README. The wrapper is always emitted, even when no bid wins, because the fragment needs
    an element of its own to remove in that case.
</iscomment>
<isscript>
    // Registered from here rather than from the fragment. A remote include renders no
    // decorator, so an assets.addCss inside it would be collected and then thrown away with
    // the fragment's own request - the stylesheet has to be asked for by the page.
    require('*/cartridge/scripts/assets').addCss('/css/topsort-sponsored.css');
</isscript>
<div class="ts-banner-slot ts-banner-slot--${tsSlotId} ${tsSlotClass || ''}">
    <isinclude url="${URLUtils.url('Topsort-Banner', 'slotId', tsSlotId, 'cgid', tsCategoryId || '', 'q', tsSearchQuery || '', 'preserveWrapper', 'true')}" />
</div>
